                  aria-label="Expand details">+</button>
          <slot name="header"></slot>
        </div>
        <slot name="intro"></slot>
        <div class="details" hidden><slot name="details"></slot></div>
        <button class="more" type="button">expand more</button>
      `;

      this.toggleButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".toggle");
      this.moreButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more");
      this.details = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".details");
    }

    connectedCallback() {
      this.toggleButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
      this.moreButton.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
    }

    toggle() {
      const expanded = this.details.hidden;
      this.details.hidden = !expanded;
      this.toggleButton.textContent = expanded ? "−" : "+";
      this.toggleButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(expanded));
      this.toggleButton.setAttribute(
        "aria-label",
        expanded ? "Collapse details" : "Expand details"
      );
      this.moreButton.hidden = expanded;
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

### Evaluation

Assistant A is much closer to the requested behavior. It uses Shadow DOM and named slots, leaves the intro visible while collapsed, hides the details, and places the “expand more” button below the intro. It also styles the header and provides a plus/minus indicator.

A does have a small interaction bug: clicking the indicator fires both its own click handler and the bubbling header click handler, toggling twice and leaving the component unchanged. Clicking elsewhere in the header or the button still works. It could also improve accessibility by using a real button for the indicator and exposing the expanded state.

Assistant B has serious functional problems. Its markup uses `@click` attributes, which are not event bindings in ordinary browser HTML, and its event handler does not update the icon or button text. The `open` attribute is set on the custom element, but the CSS checks for `[open]` on the inner `.xd-container`, so the details stay hidden. Also, the intro is placed inside content hidden by default, and the expand button is in the trigger row rather than below the intro. It copies slotted content into the shadow tree rather than using slots, and querying the children in the constructor can happen before those children are available.

Both answers contain the requested general idea, but A’s implementation is substantially more usable and faithful to the prompt. A’s indicator click bug is limited and easy to fix; B’s core expansion behavior does not work as intended.

My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

