      this.more.textContent = expanded ? "collapse" : "expand more";
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

### Evaluation

Assistant B is better. It uses actual named slots, keeps the intro visible while collapsed, places the “expand more” button below the intro, and reveals the details when expanded. One bug: clicking the square indicator triggers both its own click listener and the header’s listener, so the component toggles twice and appears not to respond. Clicking elsewhere in the header or the button does toggle it.

Assistant A has more fundamental issues. It hides the entire content area while collapsed, including the intro, and puts the button in the header rather than below the intro. Its open-state CSS checks for `open` on an internal container, but toggling sets that attribute on the custom element instead. The icon and button text also aren’t updated after toggling. Its rendered content is built by copying markup rather than using the provided slots.

B’s indicator-click bug should be fixed by removing the indicator’s separate click listener; the header listener already handles clicks on it. Despite that flaw, B is substantially closer to the requested behavior. My final verdict is Assistant B is significantly better: [[B>>A]]
